``` A structured type can require a Statement

`<name: Statement>` (see learn/statement_expressions.ore and learn/structured_types.ore) makes a structured type's shape say "this slot holds a deferred, callable expression," not a plain value.
```
Row<id: Statement> {}

r := Row<`1+2`>()
@puts "r.structure.id(): `r.structure.id()`"   # r.structure.id(): 3


``` Not idempotent — every call re-runs the wrapped expression

This is the real-world angle for a structured `Statement` member: a column default computed at insert-time instead of baked in once when the schema is declared, e.g. `created_at: Statement` around a clock call (Ore has no clock yet, so a counter stands in below). But it cuts both ways — two structured references sharing *the same* Statement value both advance the same underlying call, not independent ones.
```
seq      := 0
next_seq := `seq += 1`

row_a := Row<next_seq>()
row_b := Row<next_seq>()

@puts "row_a.structure.id(): `row_a.structure.id()`"         # row_a.structure.id(): 1
@puts "row_b.structure.id(): `row_b.structure.id()`"         # row_b.structure.id(): 2
@puts "row_a.structure.id() again: `row_a.structure.id()`"   # row_a.structure.id() again: 3 — rereading row_a still advances it


``` Captured by default

A Statement remembers the scope it was *built* in, not wherever it later happens to get called from — the same predictable behavior a normal closure gives you. Calling it later, from anywhere, still resolves `some_local_var` against the scope where the backtick literal itself was written.
```
some_local_var := 0
expensive       := `some_local_var += 1`

@puts "expensive(): `expensive()`"   # expensive(): 1


``` Caching a Statement's result with .memoize

Set `.memoize = true` and the first `()` call's result is cached and returned by every call after that, instead of re-running the expression.
```
expensive.memoize = true

@puts "expensive(): `expensive()`"   # expensive(): 2 — one real run past the previous section
@puts "expensive(): `expensive()`"   # expensive(): 2 — cached, didn't run again
@puts "some_local_var: `some_local_var`"   # some_local_var: 2


``` Opting into the caller's scope

`.use_caller_scope = true` switches a Statement from "predictable" (captured at build time) to "scope-agnostic" (resolved fresh at every call site, wherever that turns out to be) -- useful when the whole point is letting *whoever* ends up holding the Statement supply its own meaning for a name, rather than always seeing your one, fixed variable.

The scope-agnostic behavior only becomes visible when there's real ambiguity to resolve -- a name declared in more than one reachable scope. `Slacker` gives its `statement` member somewhere to actually run: `count` exists both as Slacker's own instance member *and* as an unrelated outer local, so which one wins depends entirely on the setting below.
```
Slacker {
	count := 0
	statement: Statement

	new { statement;
		./statement = statement
	}

	live_count {-> Number;
		statement()
	}
}

demo_predictable := {;
	count       := 2
	captured    := `count += 4`
	predictable := Slacker(captured)

	@puts "predictable.live_count(): `predictable.live_count()`"   # predictable.live_count(): 6
	@puts "count: `count`"                                         # count: 6 -- the outer count, mutated
}

demo_caller_scope := {;
	count          := 2
	dynamic        := `count += 4`
	dynamic.use_caller_scope = true
	scope_agnostic := Slacker(dynamic)

	@puts "scope_agnostic.live_count(): `scope_agnostic.live_count()`"   # scope_agnostic.live_count(): 4
	@puts "count: `count`"                                               # count: 2 -- untouched; Slacker's own count was mutated instead
}

demo_predictable()
demo_caller_scope()


``` Statement(other) adopts, rather than re-captures

Wrapping an existing Statement with `Statement(...)` behaves exactly like reusing it directly -- same wrapped expression, same captured scope, same settings -- not like building a brand new one captured at wherever this `Statement(...)` call happens to be written.
```
x := 1
s := `x + 1`
wrapped := Statement(s)

@puts "wrapped(): `wrapped()`"   # wrapped(): 2
